  <p>
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> The tool plays a low tone at high volume, which makes the speaker move air through the grille to help dislodge trapped water droplets or dust.
  </p>

  <h2>What the tool does: exact frequencies</h2>
  <p>
    All tones are generated directly within your web browser in real time using the client-side HTML5 Web Audio API. No
    audio files are streamed or stored, and no app installation is needed.
  </p>
  <p>The code uses specific acoustic frequencies depending on the selected mode:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Water Eject mode:</strong> Plays a low-frequency tone centered on a <strong>165&nbsp;Hz</strong> square-wave
      core, reinforced with an <strong>82.5&nbsp;Hz</strong> sub-bass layer (one octave lower) and a
      <strong>330&nbsp;Hz</strong> harmonic tone (one octave higher). A low-frequency oscillator (LFO) pulses this sound at
      <strong>3.2&nbsp;Hz</strong> through a <strong>200&nbsp;Hz</strong> low-shelf filter (+8&nbsp;dB bass boost) to
      create repeated bursts of air displacement.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dust Cleaner mode:</strong> Plays dual-band high-frequency sawtooth sweeps spanning <strong>2.5&nbsp;kHz to
      6&nbsp;kHz</strong> (mid-band) and <strong>8&nbsp;kHz to 14.5&nbsp;kHz</strong> (high-band), combined with a
      <strong>3.5&nbsp;kHz</strong> presence boost filter to vibrate the speaker grille mesh and help loosen light dust and
      lint.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stereo Diagnostic mode:</strong> Plays calibrated left-channel, right-channel, and stereo-centered tones
      to help you verify whether sound output and channel balance have returned to normal after cleaning.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How acoustic ejection works and its limits</h2>
  <p>
    The tool plays a low tone at high volume, which makes the speaker move air through the grille.
    Manufacturers use the same idea (such as Apple Watch Water Lock &mdash; see <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108351" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Apple Support 108351</a>).
    Beyond moving air at the exterior grille, playing sound does not dry internal components or repair damaged hardware.
  </p>

  <h3>Hardware limits: what the tool cannot fix</h3>
  <p>
    Playing sound only moves air at the exterior speaker grille. It cannot:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Repair internal short circuits on the logic board or electronic components.</li>
    <li>Remove water that has reached inside the battery compartment or display assembly.</li>
    <li>Fix speaker cones that are physically torn, punctured, or permanently corroded from prolonged immersion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>When to stop and seek repair service</h3>
  <p>Stop using the tool and consult an authorized service provider immediately if you observe any of the following:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The phone will not turn on, restarts repeatedly, or becomes abnormally hot to the touch.</li>
    <li>You notice battery swelling, a bulging back panel, or foggy condensation under the camera lens or display glass.</li>
    <li>A "Liquid Detected" warning persists in the charging port after a full 24 hours of drying.</li>
    <li>The speaker produces no sound at all, or remains severely crackled after multiple cleaning cycles and drying.</li>
    <li>The device was dropped in salt water, chlorinated pool water, or drinks with sugar or acid, which cause corrosion unless professionally cleaned.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How official manufacturer features compare</h2>
  <p>Device manufacturers handle moisture and liquid contact through specific built-in mechanisms and recommendations:</p>

  <h3>Apple guidance</h3>
  <p>
    On Apple Watch, Apple includes Water Lock, which prevents accidental screen taps while in water and plays a series of tones when turned off to clear water from the speaker grille (<a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108351" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Apple Support 108351</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    On iPhone, Apple's official advice for a wet phone is to tap the iPhone gently against your hand with the connector facing down to remove excess liquid, and leave it in a dry area with airflow (<a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102643" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Apple Support 102643</a>).
  </p>

  <h3>Samsung guidance</h3>
  <p>
    Water-resistant Samsung Galaxy devices display a moisture detection alert if liquid is present in the USB charging port, disabling wired charging to prevent corrosion of the charging pins (<a href="https://www.samsung.com/uk/support/mobile-devices/what-to-do-if-your-water-resistant-phone-or-tablet-detects-moisture-in-the-charging-port/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Samsung UK Support</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    Samsung notes that moisture inside the speaker grill can cause crackling or muffled sound, and advises patting the device dry with a clean, soft cloth and allowing it to dry before testing the audio again (<a href="https://www.samsung.com/africa_en/support/mobile-devices/there-is-noise-in-the-speaker-sound-of-the-device/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Samsung Support</a>).
  </p>

  <h3>Water resistance limits</h3>
  <p>
    As documented by Apple (<a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108039" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Apple Support 108039</a>), water resistance is not a permanent condition and can decrease over time as a result of normal wear. Liquid damage is not covered under standard manufacturer warranties.
  </p>

  <h2>Safety and best practices</h2>
  <p>Follow these safety guidelines when using the tool:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Volume:</strong> Set your device media volume to 100% so the speaker moves air through the grille.
      <strong>Do not hold the speaker directly against your ear</strong> while the tone is playing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Duration:</strong> If moisture remains after playing a tone, allow the phone to rest in a dry area with airflow.
      Running tones continuously for hours will not dry the device faster and will drain the battery.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Orientation:</strong> Hold the phone so the speaker grille faces downward over a dry cloth or paper towel so expelled droplets can drop away.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not use heat:</strong> Apple and Samsung both advise against using external heat sources such as hair dryers, heat guns, or ovens.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not use compressed air:</strong> Apple and Samsung both warn against using compressed air or high-pressure air guns on device openings.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not use rice:</strong> Apple explicitly warns that putting an iPhone in a bag of rice can allow small particles of rice to damage the phone. For details, read our guide on <a href="/guides/does-rice-fix-a-wet-phone">whether rice fixes a wet phone</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not insert sharp objects:</strong> Apple and Samsung both advise against inserting foreign objects, such as cotton swabs or pins, into ports or speaker grilles.
    </li>
  </ul>

